<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jutland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jutland is the peninsula of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> that forms the mainland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, together with the northern part of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>. Probably the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> who migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the 5th century, it was a core land of the early Danish kingdom. Its southern part, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, was long disputed between Denmark and the German states, and in 1916 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> off its coast saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jutland">Battle of Jutland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jutland.jpg" alt="Jutland" width="320" height="579" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LibIchtnatz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jutland_Peninsula_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Torstenson War of 1643–1645 Swedish armies overran <b>Jutland</b>, and by the Treaty of Brömsebro Denmark-Norway ceded Gotland, Ösel and the Norwegian provinces of Jämtland and Härjedalen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Danish kings appear in Frankish sources from the late eighth century: Godfred defied Charlemagne and strengthened the Danevirke, the earthwork across the base of the <b>Jutland</b> peninsula, around 808.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The geographer Ptolemy, writing around 150, places a people called Saxones in the neck of the <b>Jutland</b> peninsula, but the reading is disputed and may be a copyist’s error.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Market towns, Ribe, Hedeby at the base of <b>Jutland</b>, Birka on Lake Mälaren and Kaupang in Norway, drew craftsmen who made combs, beads, jewellery and weapons, and merchants who dealt in furs, walrus ivory, amber, slaves and silver.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>When German troops invaded South <b>Jutland</b> in February 1864, in the Second Schleswig War, none of the partners came to Denmark's aid; the hope of a united Scandinavia was shattered, Bjørnson turned from monarchist to republican, and the verse to the king was struck out.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ja-vi-elsker-dette-landet">Ja, vi elsker dette landet</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Viborg in <b>Jutland</b>, Valdemar I defeated his rival for the Danish throne, Sweyn III, who was killed while fleeing.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
